(a) Composition.
(1) Conservancy members. The conservancy shall be a standing committee of the city and shall consist of five members appointed by the mayor, by and with the advice and consent of the city council. In the event that no appointment by the mayor has been approved by council within 60 days of the occurrence of the vacancy then the council shall make said appointment(s). Members shall represent all of the neighborhoods and subdivisions within the city to the fullest extent possible.
(2) Liaison members. There may be liaison members who shall be officers and members of other committees whose duties and responsibilities are related to the conservancy.
(3) Conservancy member on board of directors of Gwinnett Open Land Trust (GOLT). The city council shall nominate an individual to fill one seat on GOLT's board of directors, with appointment of the director to be subject to approval by the GOLT board., which approval shall not be unreasonably withheld. The conservancy shall recommend a member or members to the council for such a nomination, but such recommendation(s) shall not be binding upon the council.
(b) Appointment and tenure. On or after January 1, 2004, the members of the conservancy shall be appointed pursuant to section (a)(1). At that time, three members shall be appointed for two-year terms and two members shall be appointed for a one-year term. Commencing with the appointments on or after January 1, 2005, all members shall be appointed for two-year terms. Members shall serve until their successors are appointed. Members may serve consecutive terms.
(c) Vacancies. Vacancies shall be filled by appointments for unexpired terms in the same manner as for original appointments.
(d) Removal. Members of the conservancy shall serve at the pleasure of the mayor and council, and may be removed in their discretion. Appointed members who are unable to attend meetings for two consecutive sessions may be asked by the chairperson of the conservancy concerning their future intentions and the conservancy may request that the mayor and council replace said member.
(e) Compensation. All members of the conservancy shall serve without compensation.
(f) Officers. The conservancy chairperson shall be appointed by the mayor for one calendar year or until a successor is appointed. (Amended Feb. 3, 2004)
(g) Meetings. The conservancy shall meet at least quarterly on a regularly scheduled meeting date to be determined by the members of the conservancy. The minutes and records of the meetings shall be maintained by the conservancy with copies of all such records provided to the city clerk.
(h) Powers and duties. The conservancy shall have the following specific powers and duties:
(1) To work to acquire or establish buffers and greenspace on property within the city and on Peachtree Industrial Boulevard, and surrounding the city limits;
(2) To recommend and plan the maintenance of the buffers and greenspace in these areas;
(3) To submit a site management plan to be used for the stewardship of greenspace areas under city management for approval by the city council;
(4) To recommend and coordinate projects for the creation of recreational property;
(5) To make recommendations for the planning, construction and maintenance of city parks;
(6) To provide quarterly reports and make presentations to the mayor and council on ongoing projects;
(7) To cooperate with the city in applying for funds from federal, state, or local public or quasi-public agencies and to recommend the expenditure of such funds as the council may direct and authorize by resolution;
(8) To initiate development of, and implement a site management plan for each tract of greenspace property owned by the city. A GOLT board member who is not affiliated with the city will be an ex-officio participant in this program for property where a conservation easement has been conveyed to GOLT;
(9) To serve as an advisory committee to GOLT;
(10) To report at least quarterly to the GOLT board;
(11) To assist in promoting the awareness of GOLT missions and activities within the Berkeley Lake community;
(12) To be the liaison with the Berkeley Lake City Council regarding property preserved in or adjacent to Berkeley Lake;
(13) To make certain recommendations with regard to the prohibited uses and reserved rights contained in any conservation easement regarding greenspace property owned by the city as such responsibilities may be set forth therein; and (Amended April 1, 2004)
(14) To administer any other duties delegated to it by the mayor and council.
(Ord. No. O-45-07, § 3-205.1, 4-19-2007)
Cross reference— City Charter § 2.20(b)

